<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_document" style="font-size:173%;">Exp-Force: Experience-Conditioned Pre-Grasp Force Selection with Vision-Language Models
</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Siqi Shang*
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Minchao Huang*
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Bill Fan
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Lillian Chin
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_funding ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>This work was partially supported by the Texas Robotics Industrial Affiliate Program.</span></span></span><span id="id2" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>*Equal contribution. All authors are with The University of Texas at Austin, TX, USA. Correspondence: <span id="id2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">siqi.shang@utexas.edu</span></span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Accurate pre-contact grasp force selection is critical for safe and reliable robotic manipulation. Adaptive controllers regulate force after contact but still require a reasonable initial estimate. Starting a grasp with too little force requires reactive adjustment, while starting a grasp with too high a force risks damaging fragile objects. This trade-off is particularly challenging for compliant grippers, whose contact mechanics are difficult to model analytically. We propose Exp-Force, an experience-conditioned framework that predicts the minimum feasible grasping force from a single RGB image. The method retrieves a small set of relevant prior grasping experiences and conditions a vision–language model on these examples for in-context inference, without analytic contact models or manually designed heuristics. On 129 object instances, Exp-Force achieves a best-case MAE of 0.43 <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_unit">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, reducing error by 72% over zero-shot inference. In real-world tests on 30 unseen objects, it improves appropriate force selection rate from 63% to 87%. These results demonstrate that Exp-Force enables reliable and generalizable pre-grasp force selection by leveraging prior interaction experiences.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">INTRODUCTION</span></h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Applying an appropriate amount of grasping force to an object is fundamental to successful robotic manipulation. Grasping force directly determines both grasp stability and object safety: too little force will cause the object to slip out of grasp, while too much force risks damaging fragile objects. Existing research on grasp force selection can be divided into <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">pre-grasp force selection</em> and <em id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">adaptive force control</em>. Pre-grasp methods select an initial force prior to contact based on object attributes or prior knowledge, while adaptive methods regulate force after contact using tactile feedback. Current research prefers the latter approach, as the force can be adjusted throughout the grasping process  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. However, adaptive methods still require an initial guess of force. Starting with near-zero force reduces the risk of damaging delicate objects, but requires reliable slip detection to prevent failure on heavier or low-friction objects. Starting with a higher initial force increases grasp stability but risks damaging fragile objects. Pairing adaptive methods with a <em id="S1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">pre-grasp</em> force predictor can provide an appropriate “just-enough” force estimate before contact.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Generalizable pre-grasp force prediction remains an open problem. Classical analytical approaches derive grasping forces from contact mechanics and force-closure principles <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, but rely on precise prior knowledge that is typically unavailable for unseen objects (e.g., object geometry, friction, and contact conditions). Data-driven alternatives avoid explicit modeling by learning mappings from perceptual input to grasp force as part of a control policy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. However, the generalization of such methods is inherently constrained by the diversity of object properties, interaction conditions, and gripper embodiments contained within the training dataset. The problem becomes even more challenging for compliant grippers, whose nonlinear and embodiment-dependent contact behaviors make accurate modeling particularly difficult <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.08668v1/Figure1_with_pictures.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/522;" width="476" height="522" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 1: </span><span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overview</span>. Exp-Force uses VLMs to accurately predict appropriate grasp forces using a single object-image, prior to grasp contact. We do this by providing a VLM with a small set of related experiences for in-context learning. When grasping an opened Cheez-It box, a zero-shot VLM overestimates the grasp force by 2.75x, crushing the box, while Exp-Force can lift the box without damaging it.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S1.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.08668v1/method_figure_final.png" id="S1.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/197;" width="476" height="197" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 2: </span><span id="S1.F2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exp-Force architecture.</span> A single target object image <math id="S1.F2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> from the wrist camera is first fed into the descriptor VLM to generate a text description <math id="S1.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> of the object’s physical properties related to robot grasping. Together with the object image, the text description is fed into the embedding model to obtain the object embedding <math id="S1.F2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{o}</annotation></semantics></math>. The embedding is used to perform a cosine-similarity search against the Experience Pool. The top-<math id="S1.F2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> examples are retrieved and supplied to the predictor VLM as in-context examples for estimating the grasping force <math id="S1.F2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{F}_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{F}_{o}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each example contains the object name, mass, description, image, and ground-truth <math id="S1.F2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We propose using foundation models to make pre-grasp force predictions, emulating how humans select grasp forces. Humans connect an object’s appearance to prior interactions with visually and semantically similar objects. This process generates reliable force predictions by combining knowledge gained from previous grasps with current visual context <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. We adopt a similar <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">experience-conditioned</span> approach to grasp force estimation using the knowledge encoded in visual-language models (VLMs). Large VLMs such as GPT-5.2 and Gemini-3.1-Pro encode human-like common-sense knowledge about object physical properties through massive image–text training data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. Prior work has shown that conditioning foundation models on retrieved examples through retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) can improve robotic planning and decision making by grounding model reasoning in relevant past experiences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>.
Recent work has explored leveraging foundation models for forceful grasping by first inferring latent physical attributes (e.g., weight, friction, or compliance) from semantic descriptions and then combining these estimates with analytic formulations to compute grasping forces <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. However, such approaches rely on explicit physical modeling assumptions and have been evaluated on limited object sets with rigid parallel grippers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we propose Exp-Force, an experience-conditioned framework that predicts the appropriate grasping force for an object (Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I INTRODUCTION ‣ Exp-Force: Experience-Conditioned Pre-Grasp Force Selection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). Given a single RGB image of a novel object, Exp-Force predicts the appropriate grasping force by generating a task description with a VLM, retrieving relevant experiences from a small pool of prior grasping experiences, and incorporating those examples into the prompt of a force predictor VLM. Our approach avoids injecting human-designed heuristics or analytic modeling into the prediction process, but instead maps wrist-camera object images directly to grasping force estimates. Importantly, the framework is platform-agnostic and does not depend on a specific gripper embodiment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We demonstrate through offline and real-world experiments that Exp-Force enables data-efficient and generalizable grasping force estimation in practical robotic settings. Using a modest experience pool of only 129 object grasps (<math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx</annotation></semantics></math> 1 hour of real robot data), our method achieves accurate and stable force predictions with a best-case cross-validation mean absolute error of <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.43\,$\mathrm{N}$" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.43</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_unit">N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.43\,$\mathrm{N}$</annotation></semantics></math>. The strong predictive performance of our framework suggests that the complex interaction physics between compliant grippers and diverse objects can be implicitly captured by VLMs when grounded in a small set of relevant interaction examples.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, our contributions are:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To the best of our knowledge, this is the first work to leverage retrieval-augmented VLMs for estimating appropriate grasping forces in robotic manipulation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We demonstrate the effectiveness of Exp-Force with offline evaluation on a dataset of 129 object instances. Exp-Force achieves a force prediction error of <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.43" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.43</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.43</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_unit">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, outperforming prediction of zero-shot VLMs by 72%</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We show that accurate force estimation needs only a small (<math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=6</annotation></semantics></math>) number of relevant examples, highlighting the in-context sample efficiency of Exp-Force.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We validate the generalization capability of Exp-Force through real-world robotic experiments across diverse and unseen objects using compliant robot fingers.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">5.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We release our dataset and implementation at</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.I1.i5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="http://expforcesubmission.github.io/Exp-Force-Website/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF8000;">http://expforcesubmission.github.io/Exp-Force-Website/</a></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Methods</span></h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our objective in this work is to predict the approrpiate grasping force scalar <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> when given a single wrist-camera image <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> of an object instance <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>o</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o</annotation></semantics></math>. We define <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> as the minimum grasping force needed to lift the object without slip. Grasp force is defined as the sum of the contact normal forces of two parallel gripper fingers against the object.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To achieve this objective, we formulate force prediction as an experience-conditioned inference problem: a small set of relevant prior grasping experiences is retrieved and provided to a VLM as contextual examples to estimate <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> for a query object <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>o</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o</annotation></semantics></math> (Fig. <a href="#S1.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ I INTRODUCTION ‣ Exp-Force: Experience-Conditioned Pre-Grasp Force Selection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).
Our pipeline consists of three main components: <span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(A) Object description generation</span>, <span id="S2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(B) Experience retrieval</span>, and <span id="S2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(C) Experience-conditioned force inference</span>. Steps A and C are VLM-based and use structured prompts to ensure that inference is performed under well-defined task and embodiment assumptions.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">II-A</span> </span><span id="S2.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Object Description Generation</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The first step generates a textual description of the query image to explicitly identify object attributes that are relevant to grasping force estimation. We obtain this description by constructing the following prompt for a descriptor VLM: a shared grasp-task information <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, a task-specific description instruction <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\mathrm{desc}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>desc</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\mathrm{desc}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the input query image <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{o}</annotation></semantics></math>. This construction standardizes information about the grasp task across different object instances.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The grasp-task information <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> provides contextual information to the VLM. It specifies (1) the task objective to find <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>; (2) embodiment information about the gripper, such as geometry, structure, material, and a side-view image; and (3) a visual scale reference image captured under identical camera settings to provide metric size cues. he shared context <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is provided to both the descriptor and predictor (Sec. <a href="#S2.SS3" title="II-C Experience-Conditioned Force Inference ‣ II Methods ‣ Exp-Force: Experience-Conditioned Pre-Grasp Force Selection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II-C</span></a>) to ensure consistent physical assumptions and manipulation constraints. The instruction <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\mathrm{desc}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>desc</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\mathrm{desc}}</annotation></semantics></math> explicitly directs the model to describe object properties relevant to <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. The description instruction <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\mathrm{desc}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>desc</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\mathrm{desc}}</annotation></semantics></math> specifies that the model should characterize visual and semantic attributes relevant to grasping force estimation. Importantly, no analytic force models or manually designed heuristics are embedded in the instruction prompt.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Formally, let <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{\mathrm{desc}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>desc</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{\mathrm{desc}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> be the prompt construction function that formats <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C},\tau_{\mathrm{desc}},I_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>desc</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C},\tau_{\mathrm{desc}},I_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> into a structured multi-modal prompt for the descriptor module. The descriptor VLM <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\mathrm{desc}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>desc</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\mathrm{desc}}</annotation></semantics></math> then processes the constructed prompt to generate a textual description <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> of the object’s physical properties:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{o}=g_{\mathrm{desc}}\!\left(\Pi_{\mathrm{desc}}(\mathcal{C},\tau_{\mathrm{desc}},I_{o})\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>desc</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>desc</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>desc</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{o}=g_{\mathrm{desc}}\!\left(\Pi_{\mathrm{desc}}(\mathcal{C},\tau_{\mathrm{desc}},I_{o})\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In practice, we find that the output <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{o}</annotation></semantics></math>, offers several semantically relevant characteristics about the target object <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>o</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o</annotation></semantics></math>. Example descriptions that the VLM returns include size, shape, apparent rigidity or compliance, surface characteristics, and structural cues (ex. container-like vs. solid objects). This demonstrates that <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> serves as a semantic representation that can support similarity-based retrieval in the next stage.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.08668v1/Figure3_linked.png" id="S2.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/224;" width="476" height="224" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 3: </span><span id="S2.F3.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation objects, categories, and model performance across categories.</span> <span id="S2.F3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">(A)</span> Representative objects used in our experiments, categorized by geometry, fragility, and weight. All scale bars are 20cm. <span id="S2.F3.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">(B)</span> Objects weight versus minimum feasible grasping force <math id="S2.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>. Dashed lines indicate bounds for the coefficient of friction <math id="S2.F3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> that the zero-shot model uses in its force estimation. <span id="S2.F3.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">(C)</span> Comparison of the Mean Absolute Error between zero-shot versus Exp-Force in each of the six object categories, across three vision-language models (GPT 5.2, Gemini-3-Flash, and Gemini-3.1-Pro). For each model, we chose the best <math id="S2.F3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> value from our in-context sample efficiency ablation (Sec. <a href="#S4.SS4" title="IV-D In-context Sample Efficiency and Diminishing Returns ‣ IV Offline Experiment Results ‣ Exp-Force: Experience-Conditioned Pre-Grasp Force Selection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-D</span></a>). The total count of objects within each category is also reported.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span> </span><span id="S2.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Experience Retrieval</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we use the generated object description <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> and the object image <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> to retrieve similar prior experiences to estimate <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarity is computed by embedding both the query object and prior experiences into a shared multimodal embedding space. The multimodal embedding is computed using a pretrained vision–language embedding model <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{o}=\phi(I_{o},T_{o})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{o}=\phi(I_{o},T_{o}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{o}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{o}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the embedding of the query object <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>o</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o</annotation></semantics></math>, capturing both visual and semantic features, and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is the dimensionality of the embedding space. In this work, we adopt the Qwen3-VL-Embedding-8B model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> as the embedding function <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>, for which <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=4096" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4096</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=4096</annotation></semantics></math>. We choose Qwen3 as it is specifically optimized for multimodal retrieval tasks, mapping both the visual features of the query image <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> and the semantic description <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> into a unified vector space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We then compare <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> with the embeddings of prior experiences. We maintain an experience pool:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}=\{(n_{i},m_{i},T_{i},I_{i},F_{i}^{\star})\}_{i=1}^{N}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}=\{(n_{i},m_{i},T_{i},I_{i},F_{i}^{\star})\}_{i=1}^{N},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where each experience <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of the object’s name <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, mass <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, text description <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, pre-grasp image <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and the ground truth <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{i}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{i}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> obtained from prior robotic interaction (Section. <a href="#S3.SS1" title="III-A Experience Pool Data Collection ‣ III Experimental Setup ‣ Exp-Force: Experience-Conditioned Pre-Grasp Force Selection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III-A</span></a>). We use <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> to compute the embedding of each stored element in this experience pool (<math id="S2.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>) and calculate the cosine similarity between <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and the query embedding <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{o}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{sim}(z_{o},z_{i})=\frac{z_{o}^{\top}z_{i}}{\|z_{o}\|_{2}\,\|z_{i}\|_{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>sim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>o</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{sim}(z_{o},z_{i})=\frac{z_{o}^{\top}z_{i}}{\|z_{o}\|_{2}\,\|z_{i}\|_{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We rank experiences in <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{sim}(z_{o},z_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>sim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{sim}(z_{o},z_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> and select the <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> highest ones as our retrieved experience set:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{k}(o)=\{(n_{i},m_{i},T_{i},I_{i},F_{i}^{\star})\mid i\in\mathcal{I}_{k}(o)\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{k}(o)=\{(n_{i},m_{i},T_{i},I_{i},F_{i}^{\star})\mid i\in\mathcal{I}_{k}(o)\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}_{k}(o)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}_{k}(o)</annotation></semantics></math> contains the indices of the top-<math id="S2.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> similar embeddings. <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{k}(o)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{k}(o)</annotation></semantics></math> serves as the experience context that will be used to inform the force predictor module.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">II-C</span> </span><span id="S2.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Experience-Conditioned Force Inference</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the final stage, we condition a predictor VLM on the retrieved experiences <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{k}(o)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{k}(o)</annotation></semantics></math> to estimate the appropriate grasping force <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> through in-context inference. We construct a predictor prompt that integrates four components in sequence: the shared grasp-task information <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> defined in Sec. <a href="#S2.SS1" title="II-A Object Description Generation ‣ II Methods ‣ Exp-Force: Experience-Conditioned Pre-Grasp Force Selection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II-A</span></a>, the selected prior interactions <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{k}(o)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{k}(o)</annotation></semantics></math>, a task-specific force prediction instruction <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\mathrm{pred}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>pred</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\mathrm{pred}}</annotation></semantics></math> that directs the model to infer <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the query image <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{o}</annotation></semantics></math>. The predictor VLM <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\mathrm{pred}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>pred</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\mathrm{pred}}</annotation></semantics></math> then processes this prompt to produce an experience-conditioned estimation of the scalar minimum feasible grasping force <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{F}_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{F}_{o}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{F}_{o}=g_{\mathrm{pred}}\!\left(\Pi_{\mathrm{pred}}(\mathcal{C},\mathcal{E}_{k}(o),\tau_{\mathrm{pred}},I_{o},)\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>pred</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>pred</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>pred</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{F}_{o}=g_{\mathrm{pred}}\!\left(\Pi_{\mathrm{pred}}(\mathcal{C},\mathcal{E}_{k}(o),\tau_{\mathrm{pred}},I_{o},)\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we instantiate both the descriptor <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\mathrm{desc}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>desc</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\mathrm{desc}}</annotation></semantics></math> and predictor <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\mathrm{pred}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>pred</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\mathrm{pred}}</annotation></semantics></math> using three state-of-the-art vision–language models: GPT-5.2, Gemini-3-Flash, and Gemini-3.1-Pro. We will compare these different models in Sec. <a href="#S4" title="IV Offline Experiment Results ‣ Exp-Force: Experience-Conditioned Pre-Grasp Force Selection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a> to examine robustness across different VLMs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Importantly, no analytic contact models, friction equations, or manually designed force heuristics are incorporated into the prediction prompt. This means that the whole Exp-Force pipeline operates end-to-end from visual observation alone, avoiding the need for explicit physical modeling.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=0</annotation></semantics></math>, no prior experiences are provided in the prompt, and the formulation reduces to zero-shot inference:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{F}_{o}^{(0)}=g_{\mathrm{pred}}\!\left(\Pi_{\mathrm{pred}}(\mathcal{C},\tau_{\mathrm{pred}},I_{o})\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>o</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>pred</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>pred</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>pred</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{F}_{o}^{(0)}=g_{\mathrm{pred}}\!\left(\Pi_{\mathrm{pred}}(\mathcal{C},\tau_{\mathrm{pred}},I_{o})\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">This special case enables direct comparison between zero-shot VLMs and Exp-Force, isolating the contribution of experience conditioning.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Experimental Setup</span></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the performance of Exp-Force on predicting the target grasping force <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>, we compare Exp-Force against baselines with offline and real-world experiments. We structure our evaluation around the following questions:</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Can Exp-Force enable accurate prediction of <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> of diverse unseen objects?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">How much does each component of Exp-Force contribute to the force prediction performance?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">How many in-context examples do we need to accurately predict the target force <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>?</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Experience Pool Data Collection</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Accurate evaluation of Exp-Force requires both testing across diverse objects and accurately measuring the true minimum feasible grasping force <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>. This section describes our choice of objects and method for measuring <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We first assembled a set of 129 objects categorized into 6 semantic categories. Objects were selected by combining household items from a grocery store with items from the YCB dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> to ensure a variety in geometry, weight, fragility, and surface texture (Fig. <a href="#S2.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ II-A Object Description Generation ‣ II Methods ‣ Exp-Force: Experience-Conditioned Pre-Grasp Force Selection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>A). Object categories were created by first isolating fragile objects and classifying them as Fragile–light” or Fragile–heavy” based on whether their mass was below or above 100,<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_unit">g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. The remaining objects were classified by grasp geometry. We found that most durable household items could be classified as either “Cuboid” or “Cylinder”. Of outliers, “Bottles” were a salient category. Many liquid products are packaged in slender, slippery, and heavy bottles. All remaining outliers were classified as ”Odd shapes”.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For each object, we collect real-time grasping force and slip measurements using a pair of FORTE tactile fin-ray fingers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> on a linkage-based gripper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, as well as an object RGB image (640 x 480 resolution) collected by a RealSense D435i wrist camera. The force estimation runs at 100 Hz with <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math> 10 <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{ms}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_unit">ms</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{ms}</annotation></semantics></math> latency, while slip detection runs at 500 Hz with <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math> 100 <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{ms}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_unit">ms</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{ms}</annotation></semantics></math> latency. This wrist camera captures a top-down view image before each data collection trial to serve as a pre-grasp image <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> for our experience pool. The entire end-effector assembly is attached to a Franka Emika Panda robot arm to generate a vertical lifting motion.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To accurately measure ground-truth <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> for each object, the gripper uses adaptive force control. The gripper first applies a small grasping force of 0.25 <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_unit">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{N}</annotation></semantics></math> to the object and begins to lift the object by 5 <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{c}\mathrm{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>cm</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{c}\mathrm{m}</annotation></semantics></math> at 0.5 <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{c}\mathrm{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>cm</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{c}\mathrm{m}</annotation></semantics></math>/<math id="S3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{s}</annotation></semantics></math>. During this 10 second period, for each slip event detected, the gripper tightens its grasp by <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx</annotation></semantics></math>2 <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{m}\mathrm{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>mm</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{m}\mathrm{m}</annotation></semantics></math> (inter-finger distance), which increases the grasp force. Since grasp force is only increased until there is no longer slippage, the final force measurement accurately measures the minimum grasp force <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> required for lifting the object. We performed three lifting trials for each object, and selected the median value. Finally, we ceiling-round the value to 0.25 <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_unit">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{N}</annotation></semantics></math> increments to account for sensor noise and the grasp force estimator’s RMSE (<math id="S3.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx</annotation></semantics></math> 0.2 <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{N}</annotation></semantics></math>). Before applying this approach for finding <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> to all 129 objects, we verified that <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> was accurate across 20 objects through robot teleoperation by confirming that grasp forces under <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> were insufficient to lift the object. While scaling this method to the full dataset, we found that adaptive force control failed for 10 objects (Pocky, drill, lotion bottle, Clorox toilet bleach, Oxiclean 21.5oz, mustard, sugar, bean paste 28oz, muffin mix, black AC adapter). We established the ground truth <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> for these objects through robot teleoperation instead.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ II-A Object Description Generation ‣ II Methods ‣ Exp-Force: Experience-Conditioned Pre-Grasp Force Selection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(b) plots the measured <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> in relation to object weight. We see our semantic categories correspond to distinct regions in the plot: fragile items cluster to the bottom left, having low weight and requiring low force; bottles cluster in the center being heavy and requiring moderately high forces; finally, odd shapes occupy extremes, such as the slippery peeler at the top left, and the heavy drill at the top right. This plot also enables us to compare the friction between the gripper and object against analytic friction models. Under a classical Coulomb friction model, <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lvert\textbf{F}_{\textrm{weight}}\rvert=\mu F_{\textrm{grasp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">F</mtext><mtext>weight</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mtext>grasp</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lvert\textbf{F}_{\textrm{weight}}\rvert=\mu F_{\textrm{grasp}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, different <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> values determine the slopes of lines through the origin. In Fig. <a href="#S2.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ II-A Object Description Generation ‣ II Methods ‣ Exp-Force: Experience-Conditioned Pre-Grasp Force Selection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(b) we plot lines corresponding to the typical range of <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> values selected by our zero-shot models. Since most objects lie below the <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=1</annotation></semantics></math> line, this indicates the gripper’s effective <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> is much higher than 1 on most objects. As we later discuss in Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="IV-A Comparison to Zero-Shot VLMs ‣ IV Offline Experiment Results ‣ Exp-Force: Experience-Conditioned Pre-Grasp Force Selection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-A</span></a>, this is the primary cause for our zero-shot VLMs consistently overestimating the appropriate grasp force <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Offline Experiments</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After data collection of the experience pool, we evaluate how well Exp-Force fares in pure force prediction on the dataset. We compare Exp-Force against three types of baselines: (1) zero-shot VLMs to isolate the performance benefit of retrieving prior interaction examples, (2) standard supervised learning baselines, and (3) ablations to assess the contributions of the predictor module, experience retriever, and embodiment information provided in the prompts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For Category (2), we compare Exp-Force against a representative pretrained vision encoder (DINOv3-Large <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>) and a representative multi-modal encoder (the previously-used Qwen3-VL-Embedding-8B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> from Sec. <a href="#S2.SS2" title="II-B Experience Retrieval ‣ II Methods ‣ Exp-Force: Experience-Conditioned Pre-Grasp Force Selection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II-B</span></a>) to incorporate object descriptions <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{o}</annotation></semantics></math>. In both cases, the encoder parameters are frozen, and a two-layer MLP head is trained on top of the extracted features to predict the <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>. We perform an extensive hyperparameter sweep over hidden dimension, dropout rate, learning rate, and weight decay (512 configurations per model). The results reported in Fig. <a href="#S3.F5" title="Fig. 5 ‣ III-C Real World Experiments ‣ III Experimental Setup ‣ Exp-Force: Experience-Conditioned Pre-Grasp Force Selection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> correspond to the best-performing checkpoints from the hyperparameter sweep on the validation splits, providing an optimistic estimate of achievable performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For Category (3), first we evaluated a simple baseline that averages the ground-truth forces <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{i}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{i}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> of the top-<math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> retrieved examples (<span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Exp Force Avg</span>). Second, we replace the retrieved set <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{k}(o)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{k}(o)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> randomly sampled experiences (<span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Random Exp</span>). Lastly, we remove the gripper embodiment information from both the descriptor and predictor prompt (<span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">w/o embodiment</span>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond the study of model components, we conduct an additional ablation study on the number of selected samples <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> to understand how the size of the retrieved set <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{k}(o)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{k}(o)</annotation></semantics></math> affects prediction performance. Specifically, we vary <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and measure the resulting force estimation accuracy. This experiment characterizes how performance scales with the quantity of conditioning examples and provides insight into the in-context sample efficiency of Exp-Force.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For all offline experiments, we evaluate Exp-Force using 5-fold cross-validation across the entire collected dataset of 129 object instances to assess generalization to unseen objects. In each fold, the available objects are partitioned into a set of query objects for force prediction and a remaining set used to construct the experience pool <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math>. Across the five folds, every object is evaluated as a query, ensuring comprehensive coverage of the dataset while guaranteeing that no query instance ever appears in its corresponding retrieved set.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.08668v1/Real_Setup.png" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/268;" width="476" height="268" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 4: </span><span id="S3.F4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Real-world experiment setup.</span>
<span id="S3.F4.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(A)</span> The compliant tactile two-finger gripper with a wrist-mounted camera used for pre-grasp image capture and force execution.
<span id="S3.F4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(B)</span> Five selected objects from each of the six categories. All scale bars are 10cm. </figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">III-C</span> </span><span id="S3.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Real World Experiments</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate Exp-Force in the real world, we grasp objects with the force estimation <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{F}_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{F}_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> given by Exp-Force and the zero-shot VLM baseline (Fig. <a href="#S3.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ III-B Offline Experiments ‣ III Experimental Setup ‣ Exp-Force: Experience-Conditioned Pre-Grasp Force Selection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>A). We perform our real-world evaluations on the same grasping platform we used to collect the experience pool dataset (Sec. <a href="#S3.SS1" title="III-A Experience Pool Data Collection ‣ III Experimental Setup ‣ Exp-Force: Experience-Conditioned Pre-Grasp Force Selection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III-A</span></a>). We compare the performance of Exp-Force and zero-shot VLM over 150 grasps: 5 grasp trials each for 30 objects. The thirty objects were chosen by selecting five objects with different sizes, textures, and weights from each of the six categories described in Sec. <a href="#S3.SS1" title="III-A Experience Pool Data Collection ‣ III Experimental Setup ‣ Exp-Force: Experience-Conditioned Pre-Grasp Force Selection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III-A</span></a>, and are shown in Fig. <a href="#S3.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ III-B Offline Experiments ‣ III Experimental Setup ‣ Exp-Force: Experience-Conditioned Pre-Grasp Force Selection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>B. We chose Gemini-3-flash as the selected VLM based on our results in Sec. <a href="#S4" title="IV Offline Experiment Results ‣ Exp-Force: Experience-Conditioned Pre-Grasp Force Selection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a>. Although our offline experiments showed that Gemini-3-pro is our most performant model, we found Gemini-3-pro exhibited inconsistent response latency under high API load during our experiments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In each grasp trial, we first perturb the pose of the target object, and then take an image with the robot wrist camera from a consistent end-effector pose. We then use this image to query both a zero-shot VLM and Exp-Force (<math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=7</annotation></semantics></math>) for their pre-grasp force estimates. Finally, for each of the two force outputs, we manually center the target object under the gripper and perform a grasp. To simplify the real-world experiments, trials whose outcomes could be inferred from previous trials were not repeated. For example, if an object was crushed at 5 <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_unit">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, we did not perform further trials on the same object with larger forces. Grasp forces with inconsistent grasp outcomes were re-run three additional times, with the more common outcome taken as the final outcome.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.08668v1/vlm_mae_rmse_grouped.svg" id="S3.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/147;" width="476" height="147"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 5: </span>Comparison of force prediction errors across three vision–language models (GPT-5.2, Gemini-3-flash, and Gemini-3.1-pro). Lower values correspond to more accurate force estimation. For the multimodal baseline, we utilize Qwen3-VL-Embedding (denoted as Qwen). For each model, distribution bars summarize the error statistics over five-fold cross-validation, where the thin whiskers denote one standard deviation.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S3.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.08668v1/mae_vs_k_with_errorbars.svg" id="S3.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/357;" width="476" height="357"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 6: </span>Effect of the retrieved example size (<math id="S3.F6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>) on force estimation error. Our evaluation reveals that Exp-Force exhibits remarkable in-context sample efficiency. The plot demonstrates that, across all predictor VLMs, the Mean Absolute Error (MAE) rapidly converges as the model switches from zero-shot inference (<math id="S3.F6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=0</annotation></semantics></math>) to experience-conditioned inference. Performance plateaus early, typically between <math id="S3.F6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=5</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.F6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=10</annotation></semantics></math>, highlighting the high sample efficiency of Exp-Force. Shaded regions denote the standard deviation across cross-validation folds, illustrating that the Gemini models maintain superior estimation stability and tighter error bounds compared to GPT-5.2.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We categorize the grasp outcome as Appropriate, Overestimate, or Insufficient. A prescribed force is Insufficient if a grasp cannot be stably maintained, manifesting as either an initial failure to pick up the object, or the object slipping out of the grippers within one minute of lifting. If a grasp is successful, its force is categorized as an Overestimate if the commanded force is greater than <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="3\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times</annotation></semantics></math> the ground-truth <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>, or by <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>N. Otherwise, if a grasp is successful and the force is not overestimated, then it is classified as Appropriate. We choose to identify Overestimates as a standalone category because excessive grasp force is undesirable even if it does not lead to damage to the grasped object. Excessive force induces unnecessary wear on the gripper and tactile sensors. Also, fragile objects may appear deceptively non-fragile, such as the opened Cheez-It box in Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I INTRODUCTION ‣ Exp-Force: Experience-Conditioned Pre-Grasp Force Selection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Offline Experiment Results</span></h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Comparison to Zero-Shot VLMs</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, our results show that incorporating selected prior examples into the prompt substantially improves force prediction accuracy compared to zero-shot inference. Exp-Force consistently reduces error across all three tested VLMs and all six object categories (Fig. <a href="#S2.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ II-A Object Description Generation ‣ II Methods ‣ Exp-Force: Experience-Conditioned Pre-Grasp Force Selection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>C, <a href="#S3.F5" title="Fig. 5 ‣ III-C Real World Experiments ‣ III Experimental Setup ‣ Exp-Force: Experience-Conditioned Pre-Grasp Force Selection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>-No-Exp). Specifically, the mean absolute error (MAE) decreases from 1.86 <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_unit">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{N}</annotation></semantics></math> to 0.56 <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_unit">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{N}</annotation></semantics></math> for GPT-5.2, from 1.14 <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_unit">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{N}</annotation></semantics></math> to 0.46 <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_unit">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{N}</annotation></semantics></math> for Gemini-3-Flash, and from 1.52 <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_unit">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{N}</annotation></semantics></math> to 0.43 <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_unit">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{N}</annotation></semantics></math> for Gemini-3.1-Pro. The consistently higher root-mean squared error (RMSE) compared to MAE indicates the presence of larger errors on objects requiring higher <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>. These results show that incorporating selected prior examples into the prompt substantially improves force prediction accuracy compared to zero-shot VLMs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To understand the mechanisms driving the accuracy improvement over zero-shot VLMs, we qualitatively analyzed the reasoning traces from the predictors <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\mathrm{pred}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>pred</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\mathrm{pred}}</annotation></semantics></math>. While the VLMs usually successfully recognized the objects, the zero-shot <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\mathrm{pred}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>pred</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\mathrm{pred}}</annotation></semantics></math> consistently defaults to explicit analytical modeling. For example, when predicting <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> for a 236 <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_unit">g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{g}</annotation></semantics></math> Cheez-It crackers box (Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I INTRODUCTION ‣ Exp-Force: Experience-Conditioned Pre-Grasp Force Selection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), the <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\mathrm{pred}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>pred</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\mathrm{pred}}</annotation></semantics></math> recognizes the object but hallucinates unobservable physical parameters (e.g., guessing a friction coefficient of <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\approx 0.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\approx 0.6</annotation></semantics></math>) and applies a rigid-body Coulomb friction model (<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\text{grip}}\geq W/\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mtext>grip</mtext></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\text{grip}}\geq W/\mu</annotation></semantics></math>). This analytical approach is fundamentally brittle as the simplified modeling equations fail to capture the deformation and form-closure of compliant fin-ray fingers (Sec. <a href="#S3.SS1" title="III-A Experience Pool Data Collection ‣ III Experimental Setup ‣ Exp-Force: Experience-Conditioned Pre-Grasp Force Selection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III-A</span></a>). As a result, the zero-shot VLMs overestimate the required force to be 5.5 <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_unit">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Exp-Force circumvents this failure mode by allowing <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\mathrm{pred}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>pred</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\mathrm{pred}}</annotation></semantics></math> to act as a semantic-physical interpolator rather than a flawed physics engine. In the Cheez-It box example, Exp-Force’s <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\mathrm{pred}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>pred</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\mathrm{pred}}</annotation></semantics></math>
specifically cited the examples of a muffin box at 612g and a toothpaste box of 133g to make the prediction, as they share <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“similar flat, smooth cardboard properties”</span> to the target Cheez-It cracker box. It also implicitly reasons about complex contact mechanics, stating that the force <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“accounts for the load and ensures the fin-ray fingers conform well enough without
causing structural damage to the box.”</span> These results indicate that VLMs can implicitly account for hard-to-model physical interactions when grounded by a relevant set of real-world examples.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Comparison to Supervised Baselines</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Exp-Force also outperforms the standard supervised learning baselines of DINOv3-Large and Qwen3-VL-Embedding-8B (Fig. <a href="#S3.F5" title="Fig. 5 ‣ III-C Real World Experiments ‣ III Experimental Setup ‣ Exp-Force: Experience-Conditioned Pre-Grasp Force Selection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>-Baselines).
While these models achieve reasonable mean absolute errors (<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.50</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_unit">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.51" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.51</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.51</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_unit">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, with the latter using <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> from Gemini-3-Flash),
Exp-Force paired with Gemini-3.1-Pro or Gemini-3-Flash outperforms them by <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="14\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>14</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">14\%</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8\%</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. This suggests that conditioning the VLM on retrieved interaction examples for in-context inference enables more adaptive force estimation than regression based solely on distances in a frozen embedding space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This advantage over supervised baselines reveals a key difference in task formulation. Standard data-driven approaches attempt to learn a global mapping from observation directly to a continuous grasping force value, which can be prone to overfitting given our modest dataset of 129 object instances. This is because the <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> varies substantially across diverse objects as gripper-surface interactions differ. Exp-Force avoids this overfitting by reformulating the problem from absolute continuous regression to local comparative reasoning.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">IV-C</span> </span><span id="S4.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Ablation Studies</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We isolate the contributions of each component of Exp-Force for ablation studies (Fig. <a href="#S3.F5" title="Fig. 5 ‣ III-C Real World Experiments ‣ III Experimental Setup ‣ Exp-Force: Experience-Conditioned Pre-Grasp Force Selection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>-Ablations). Exp-Force achieves lower errors than the <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Exp Force Avg</span> baseline across all three selected VLMs, demonstrating that <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\mathrm{pred}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>pred</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\mathrm{pred}}</annotation></semantics></math> is actively reasoning about different object properties, rather than simply interpolating the aggregated examples. The <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Random Exp</span> baseline also performs uniformly worse across all VLMs, confirming that the performance of Exp-Force relies on the specific semantic and physical relevance of the retrieved embeddings <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, not just the structural format of the prompt.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Surprisingly, the <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">w/o embodiment</span> baseline performs only marginally worse than Exp-Force. Across all three tested VLMs, the largest increase in mean absolute error when removing the embodiment information is only <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.034\,$\mathrm{N}$" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.034</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_unit">N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.034\,$\mathrm{N}$</annotation></semantics></math>. This suggests that the predictor <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\mathrm{pred}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>pred</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\mathrm{pred}}</annotation></semantics></math> implicitly deduces the embodiment constraints through in-context inference directly from the retrieved experience set <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{k}(o)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{k}(o)</annotation></semantics></math>; i.e., the in-context examples effectively act as an implicit, data-driven physical model of the hardware. The necessity for explicit text-based embodiment hints is diminished, because the ground-truth forces <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{i}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{i}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> provided in the examples inherently encapsulate the complex contact mechanics of the specific gripper.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">IV-D</span> </span><span id="S4.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In-context Sample Efficiency and Diminishing Returns</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To understand how many prior experiences are needed for effective in-context inference, we vary the number of retrieved examples <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and measure the force prediction error. Our evaluation reveals that Exp-Force exhibits remarkable in-context sample efficiency (Fig. <a href="#S3.F6" title="Fig. 6 ‣ III-C Real World Experiments ‣ III Experimental Setup ‣ Exp-Force: Experience-Conditioned Pre-Grasp Force Selection with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>). The rapid reduction in estimation error from zero-shot (<math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=0</annotation></semantics></math>) to experience-conditioned inference suggests a fundamental shift in the predictor VLM’s reasoning mechanism when anchored by the retrieved set <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{k}(o)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{k}(o)</annotation></semantics></math>. Performance plateaus early, typically between <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=5</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=10</annotation></semantics></math>. Within this optimal data-efficient window, Gemini-3.1-Pro achieves the lowest MAE (<math id="S4.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.43\,\pm\,0.13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.43</mn><mo lspace="0.392em">±</mo><mn> 0.13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.43\,\pm\,0.13</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_unit">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{N}</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=6</annotation></semantics></math>), closely followed by Gemini-3-Flash (<math id="S4.SS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.46\,\pm\,0.16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.46</mn><mo lspace="0.392em">±</mo><mn> 0.16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.46\,\pm\,0.16</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_unit">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{N}</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=7</annotation></semantics></math>) and GPT-5.2 (<math id="S4.SS4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.56\,\pm\,0.16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.56</mn><mo lspace="0.392em">±</mo><mn> 0.16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.56\,\pm\,0.16</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_unit">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{N}</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=7</annotation></semantics></math>). Expanding the retrieved experience set beyond <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=10</annotation></semantics></math> yields diminishing returns. As a result, the models require only a small, highly relevant set of semantic neighbors to accurately anchor their estimations of <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. This diminishing return implies that once a sufficient semantic neighborhood is established, adding more distant experiences provides no additional insights while diluting the prompt’s context.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F7" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.08668v1/Fig7.png" id="S4.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/312;" width="476" height="312" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 7: </span><span id="S4.F7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Snapshots of representative trials</span>. We compare grasp outcomes of a Pringle chip, a champagne glass, and a banana, using forces generated by both our zero-shot baseline and Exp-Force with Gemini-3-flash and k=7 from the same image. The chip grasp is representative of a typical successful grasp. The Champagne Glass and Banana correspond respectively to typical failure modes - the zero-experience model overestimating, and our method under-estimating. All scale bars are 5cm.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
